# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Escort_(Europe)
# Ford Escort Mk2: the squared-off Escort of December 1974 - August 1980,
# launched in the UK in January 1975 (Wikipedia, "Ford Escort (Europe)",
# second-generation section: the first production cars left the line on
# 2 December 1974, the range was announced in January 1975, and the Mark I
# overlapped in production until March 1975). The Mk2 used the Mk I's floorpan,
# running gear and core structure under a new, much squarer body developed
# jointly by Ford of Britain and Ford of Germany (development code "Brenda").
#
# The Escort was the UK's second best-selling car in every year of the Mk2's
# life, behind the Ford Cortina. UK registrations (SMMT data via AROnline,
# "Britain's best-selling cars 1965-1999"):
#   1975 Escort 103,817 (Cortina 106,787)     1978 Escort 114,415 (Cortina 139,204)
#   1976 Escort 124,166 (Cortina 126,238)     1979 Escort 131,667 (Cortina 192,184)
#   1977 Escort 103,389 (Cortina 120,601)     1980 Escort 122,357 (Cortina 190,281)
# Total Escort registrations over 1975-80 were 699,811 against the Cortina's
# 875,295, a ratio of 0.80. The Mk2 is the volume saloon; the Sport, Mexico,
# RS1800 and RS2000 rally variants are not modelled.
#
# Dimensions: Wikipedia/Autocar 1975 second-generation figures: wheelbase 94.5 in
# (2,400 mm); overall length 156.5 in (3,975 mm) saloon (estate 163 in,
# 4,140 mm); width 61.8 in (1,570 mm); height 55.65 in (1,414 mm); kerb weight
# 1,940 lb (880 kg).
#
# Colours: Diamond White, Venetian Red, Bermuda Blue, Signal Yellow and Roman
# Bronze are real Ford of Britain paint names for the Escort Mk2, taken from the
# period Ford colour cards of May 1976, January 1978 and July 1979 (reproduced at
# rsmk2.com, "RS2000 Colours"), cross-checked against the Ford Escort Mk2 colour
# list at classiccarhub.co.uk (Ford classic paint colour codes: Diamond White B
# 1975-80, Venetian Red 1975-78, Bermuda Blue 1977, Signal Yellow 1975-80, Roman
# Bronze S/F6 1975-78 metallic) and against the Ford paint codes published by
# Zero Paints for the Mk2 Escort (Signal Yellow 9/KL, Venetian Red R/JM, Diamond
# White B/3/FB, Roman Bronze S/F6). Availability used for the intro staggering:
# Diamond White 1973-1980 (a Ford standard colour from 1973), Venetian Red
# 1975/76-1980 (the 1975-76 launch red was Carnival Red, superseded by Venetian
# Red; one red is modelled across the run), Bermuda Blue 1976-1979, Signal Yellow
# 1976-1980, Roman Bronze 1976-1978 (metallic). Carnival Red, Signal Orange,
# Signal Green, Peppermint Green, Calypso Green, Daytona Yellow, Java Green,
# Terracotta, Midnight Blue, Nordic Blue, Riviera Blue, Cordoba Beige, Tuscan
# Beige and the rest of the catalogue are omitted as the less common of the range.
#
# Colour shares: the DVSA first-registration colour table starts with 1994
# registrations, so its measured base for cars first registered in 1994-96 (blue
# 23-25%, red 19-23%, white 7-10%, black 8-10%, grey 4-5%, silver 9-11%, other
# 20-26%) is the starting point, adjusted for mid-to-late-1970s registrations:
# the measured blue:red ratio (about 1.2:1) is kept; white is raised from the
# measured 7-10% to about 19% of the modelled set (white was far commoner on
# 1970s registrations than among 1990s survivors); yellow and bronze/brown are
# drawn from the measured "other" group, which was at its largest in the 1970s
# (the decade of Signal Yellow and Roman Bronze); silver, black and grey, all
# minor on 1970s registrations and much larger by 1994, are not modelled.
# Shares used: blue 31%, red 26%, white 19%, yellow 12%, bronze 12%.
#
# DistributionWeight totals 440 across the five colourways, provisionally. The
# 1975/1980 parc anchor is the Ford Cortina (500 on the 2026-11-15 rebase); the
# Escort Mk2 is a strong non-anchor set just below the anchor, at 88% of it:
# over 1975-80 the Escort's UK registrations were 80% of the Cortina's and the
# Escort was second in the best-seller table in all six years, within 3-20% of
# the Cortina in each; on the parc measure the gap closes late in the period,
# because the anchor object stands for the whole Cortina range whereas the
# Escort is split into generations, and the Mk2 alone carried the youngest and
# largest Escort cohorts by 1979-80. Provisional pending the central rebase.
# The Mk1 (in tree, ford-escort-mk1.dat), the Mk2 and the Mk3 overlap in road
# presence - the Mk2 overlaps both - so the family's combined weight exceeds the
# anchor's 500 in those windows; that is a property of the per-generation
# convention and is for the central rebase to normalise.
#
# Speed 123 km/h: a game-balance figure, not the model's top speed. It sits one
# step above the Mk1 (120) and below the Mk3 (127) in the tree. Comfort 60/420 is
# one modest step above the Mk1's 55/413: the Mk2 is the same platform with a new
# body, better noise suppression and upgraded trim, but no new suspension design;
# it is well below the Cortina's 80/450 and above the Fiesta Mk1's 50/405.
# Tolerable = 330 + 1.5 x comfortable, rounded to 5.
#
# Dates describe when each colour is seen regularly on UK roads, not production
# dates. Production ended in Britain in August 1980, although Mk1-based cars
# continued in some overseas markets; road presence tails to about 1986-90,
# staggered by colour by observed road presence - white and blue longest as the
# fleet and volume solids, then red, then the period brights (yellow) and the
# metallic (bronze) shortest. Future retire dates are estimates.
#
# Rendered with the parametric car_builder.py in the blends repository. The Mk2
# has a slim chromed bumper, applied as a post-build material and Z-scale edit in
# the Blender 4.5 session (the bmc1100 and Anglia procedure).

obj=citycar
